  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5x-1} & ={7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{5x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({7}^{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)-x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ x(5\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)}{5\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
